  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nFriday, a mid level ridge is progged to lie to our east, centered \nnear East Texas, while an upper trough is positioned over the \nwestern US. Situated between these two features, southwesterly flow \naloft continues to bring moisture over our region. Disturbances will \nalso be embedded within the flow. At the surface, a trough looks to \nbe oriented NE-SW across the Texas Panhandle and SE NM, aiding to \ndraw in Gulf moisture. As a result, isolated to widespread showers \nand storms continue Friday, with rain chances ranging between 10-80% \nareawide. The best (50-80%) chances will favor the northern/ \nnorthwestern Permian Basin, SE NM, and the higher terrain in West \nTexas Friday. Rain chances decrease with southeastern progression, \nnearer the ridge core. We will still need to monitor for the \npotential of flash flooding Friday, mainly in our western counties, \ngiven there will have been several days of wetting rains by that \npoint. Shower/storm activity then tapers down overnight Friday as a \nshortwave within the trough passes over the Rockies into the central \nPlains. The shortwave dampens the ridge and flow aloft becomes zonal \nby Saturday evening. Low to medium (10-50%) rain chances shift south \nof the Pecos River Saturday afternoon, with the best chances \nremaining in the vicinity of the Davis Mountains. Along with \ndecreasing rain chances, temperatures gradually increase over the \nweekend. Highs Friday are forecast to reach the 80s at most \nlocations, then cool to the 60s and 70s overnight. Saturday's highs \ntop out in the 80s within the western portions of our region, with \n90s in our eastern portions.\n\nUpper ridging builds back over our region Sunday into Monday, ahead \nof another trough progged to approach the region Monday evening. \nRain chances decrease further over the higher terrain Sunday \nafternoon and highs generally reach the 90s (80s in/near the higher \nterrain). With the approach of our next storm system, rain chances \nincrease again Monday evening into Tuesday, again raising \nconcerns for heavy rainfall and flash flooding. Of course, details\nshall be ironed out over the coming days, so stay tuned!",
